\citation{nguyen2020hpa}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Standard Kubernetes HPA feedback loop architecture, reproduced from Nguyen et al.~\cite  {nguyen2020hpa}. The HPA controller operates as a discrete-time proportional regulator: it periodically reads aggregated resource metrics from the API server (sourced either from the Metrics Server for KRM or the Prometheus Adapter for PCM) and computes a desired replica count using Equation~\ref {eq:hpa}. A critical architectural constraint is that this loop assumes the observed metric faithfully represents current workload intensity - an assumption that is violated for persistent-connection workloads where connections persist independently of CPU utilization.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hpa_arch}{{1}{5}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces KRM baseline: Observed CPU utilization over time. Significant overshoot above the 60\% target threshold occurs at burst onset, reflecting the inherent actuation delay of the discrete-time proportional control loop.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:krm_cpu}{{2}{5}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}Prometheus Custom Metrics (PCM) Pipeline}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{nguyen2020hpa}
\citation{nguyen2020hpa}
\citation{nguyen2020hpa}
\citation{nguyen2020hpa}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces KRM baseline: Divergence between \texttt  {desiredReplicas} (as computed by HPA) and \texttt  {currentReplicas} (as observed in the deployment) across scale-up events. The gap quantifies the combined actuation delay attributable to pod scheduling latency and container startup time.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:krm_desired}{{3}{6}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces PCM baseline: Effect of Prometheus scrape interval (15~s, 30~s, 60~s) on HPA scaling responsiveness. A 60~s scrape interval introduces visible staircase delay that significantly degrades scale-up latency; reducing to 30~s yields a substantial improvement, while the marginal gain from 30~s to 15~s is comparatively small - consistent with the findings of Nguyen et al.~\cite  {nguyen2020hpa}.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pcm_scrape}{{4}{6}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}The HPA Control Architecture}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
